Third-party attribution
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

AppTrace's design was informed by a research spike over two Apache-2.0 licensed
open-source projects. No source code was copied from either. The concepts that
were adopted, adapted or rejected are documented in docs/RESEARCH.md, and this
file records the attribution.

1. Bulk Crap Uninstaller (BCU)
   https://github.com/BCUninstaller/Bulk-Crap-Uninstaller
   Copyright Marcin Szeniak and contributors
   Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

   Concepts adopted or adapted (see docs/RESEARCH.md section 1):
     - a signed evidence score with explicit, tabulated weights, in which
       evidence can also reduce confidence
       (source/UninstallTools/Junk/Confidence/ConfidenceRecords.cs,
        source/UninstallTools/Junk/Confidence/ConfidenceCollection.cs)
     - mapping an evidence score onto a small coarse enum rather than exposing a
       numeric confidence
       (source/UninstallTools/Junk/Confidence/ConfidenceLevel.cs)
     - treating certain directory names as carrying no identity information
       (source/UninstallTools/UninstallToolsGlobalConfig.cs)
     - penalising a candidate location that another installed application
       already uses
       (source/UninstallTools/Junk/Finders/JunkCreatorBase.cs)

   Concepts deliberately not adopted: fuzzy (Sift4) name matching, single-owner
   result objects without sizes, and explanation as a preformatted string.

2. Jharu
   https://github.com/riponcm/Jharu
   Copyright riponcm and contributors
   Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

   Concepts adopted or adapted (see docs/RESEARCH.md section 2):
     - treating an unreadable location as a lower bound on size and reporting it,
       rather than silently reporting zero
       (src-tauri/src/scanner.rs)
     - tracking an exclusive byte count separately from a total so shared data is
       never double counted, and documenting the difference
       (src-tauri/src/models.rs)
     - a reliability signal that licenses the tool to decline to make a claim
       (src-tauri/src/models.rs)
     - not nesting parallel work around an already-parallel directory walk
       (src-tauri/src/scanner.rs)

   Concepts deliberately not adopted: hard-coded application/path rule tables,
   identity from normalized-name substring matching, single-boolean confidence,
   and enforcing the trust boundary in the UI layer.

